#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429} :activity/id #uuid "f4d9b1d3-776c-5de2-b62c-cbb016b96f1e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048726 :topic/id #uuid "b8f25367-71a5-50d2-9e6e-d239937a1fac" :topic/math-academy-id 1972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125794 :tutorial/content "The characteristic equation of a square matrix $A$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the left-hand side of this equation, we have the expression \n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I),\n$$\n\n which we call the **characteristic polynomial** of the matrix $A.$\n\nFor instance, let's find the characteristic polynomial of the matrix ![](images/1d/1d6d0bdedbb25bbc41759e443cdc0f6a207ef5cbfa4761b05e1a1a2eda24cbec.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(C-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =(-8-\\lambda )\\cdot (7-\\lambda )-(-9)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "25396b96-19ec-592d-b1c4-3c6fa172ef08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3580 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9083e01a-4e57-589b-9b08-48ecc4af0075" :step/math-academy-id 20133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125795 :tutorial/content "The **Cayley-Hamilton theorem** states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $\\,n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix whose characteristic polynomial is* \n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I) = (-1)^n\\lambda^n + c_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1\\lambda + c_0.\n$$\n\n*Then, the matrix $A$ is a solution of the characteristic equation $p(\\lambda) = 0$.* \n\nThat is to say, if we substitute $A$ into the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$, taking $A^0 = I,$ then we have\n\n$$\np(A) = (-1)^nA^n + c_{n-1}A^{n-1}+\\cdots+c_1A + c_0I = O,\n$$\n\nwhere $O$ is the zero-matrix. \n\nFor example, according to this theorem, for the matrix \n![](images/d2/d24f480293267a9ddc28b03626e7bde6f01bd62a136fed17f8580bf1ea34d776.svg)\nwhose characteristic polynomial is \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2 +\\lambda -2,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 +A -2I =O.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86101838-75af-5a3f-9dae-1e63a94c77a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3581 :tutorial/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7b3b4f95-8159-5533-bd80-db194716f75b" :step/math-academy-id 20134 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430412}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"} :step/id #uuid "d9e0bce9-63bf-58e4-af9b-2bb5b5d8e03c" :step/math-academy-id 20135 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124346 :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430413}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"} :step/id #uuid "1c6e6482-d051-50c1-b91d-40d9a95a380d" :step/math-academy-id 20136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125796 :tutorial/content "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1c54b7a6-10ea-5c57-ad4e-245a308d4790" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3582 :tutorial/title "The Characteristic Polynomial for 2x2 Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "4e500723-739c-51c7-b82e-8727a24a1b1f" :step/math-academy-id 20137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124347 :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050117}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "0fed3d43-139d-505f-8482-70f3be0516d6" :step/math-academy-id 20138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124361 :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050669}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430411}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f3faf291-dd20-56c9-bc3d-8482113b6764" :step/math-academy-id 21546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125824 :tutorial/content "If we want to evaluate a high-degree polynomial for a given matrix input, it can take a lot of time. For example, suppose we wanted to evaluate the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^{10} - 5x^9 - 2x^8 + x + 2\n$$\n\nfor the input ![](images/0f/0f9bff826261a956305962b4ca945ed91012cc5042e6ffe92a4b5f1ce13d3237.svg) Computing the $10$th power of this matrix would be a lot of work!\n\nLuckily, there is a way to speed up the process. The trick is to use Cayley-Hamilton theorem, which tells us that every matrix is a solution to its characteristic equation.\n\nFor the given matrix ![](images/65/6514096b22aed47005e5ff59c47e3cec701aed5b120b0022b186f1bec941d953.svg) the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-6 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the Cayley-Hamilton theorem tells us that\n\n$$\np(A) = A^2 - 5A - 2I = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, observe that the polynomial $f(x)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{10}-5{x}^{9}-2{x}^{8}+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}({x}^{2}-5x-2)+x+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{8}p(x)+x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = A^8 p(A) + A + 2I,\n$$\n\nand because $p(A) = 0,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{8}\\cdot 0+A+2I \\\\  & =0+A+2I \\\\  & =A+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the computation is much faster:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =A+2I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Other times, it may not be so obvious how to group the terms of $f(x)$ to reveal $p(x).$ In the general case, it's best to divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division, and use the result\n\n$$\nf(x) = q(x) p(x) + r(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is the quotient and $r(x)$ is the remainder. Let's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "769ceb10-1667-52b6-8a89-b4c7ca835276" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4448 :tutorial/title "Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem to Quickly Evaluate a Matrix Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "3167e19f-42e7-5fc7-8ee9-fb73a5fa3da3" :step/math-academy-id 27370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125434}}] :activity/title "The Cayley-Hamilton Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124757}]}
